= content_for(:page_title) do
  Component Linting

- # Rows for the "what gets flagged" table: the markup, whether the rule
- # fires, and why.
- verdicts = []
- verdicts << [".card", true, "`card` is the root class `daisy_card` declares."]
- verdicts << [".card-body", true, "Reported as `daisy_card (body part)` — the part classes belong to the component too."]
- verdicts << [".btn.btn-primary", true, "Only `.btn` is reported. `btn-primary` is a modifier and goes in `css:`."]
- verdicts << ["%span.badge-success", false, "A modifier on its own is not hand-rolled markup."]
- verdicts << [".flex.gap-4.p-4", false, "Tailwind utilities are never flagged — utilities in a view are correct usage."]
- verdicts << ["%div{ class: card_classes }", false, "Computed values are not the rule's to judge; guessing would only produce false positives."]

.max-w-prose
  = doc_info(url: "https://github.com/profoundry-us/loco_motion/blob/main/lib/loco_motion/lint/component_map.rb", icon: "magnifying-glass", icon_css: "text-primary") do
    :markdown
      _Loco_**Motion** ships a lint rule for HAML and ERB that catches views
      hand-rolling DaisyUI markup the library already owns — and tells you
      which helper to call instead.

.prose
  :markdown
    # Linting Component Usage

    Views compose components; components own markup. That single sentence is
    the premise the whole library rests on, and since 0.8.0 it is something
    you can *enforce* rather than merely agree with. This guide covers why the
    rule exists, how to wire it up for HAML or ERB, exactly what it does and
    does not flag, and how to adopt it on a codebase that already has a
    backlog.

- # The roadmap: five sections plus the victory lap. The links opt out of
- # Turbo because the layout wraps pages in a turbo-frame, which would
- # otherwise swallow same-page anchor jumps.
.mt-8.rounded-box.border.border-base-300.p-6
  = daisy_steps(css: "w-full steps-vertical sm:steps-horizontal") do |steps|
    - steps.with_step(css: "step-primary") do
      %a{ href: "#why", class: "link-hover", data: { turbo: "false" } } Why
    - steps.with_step(css: "step-primary") do
      %a{ href: "#setup", class: "link-hover", data: { turbo: "false" } } Setup
    - steps.with_step(css: "step-primary") do
      %a{ href: "#flagged", class: "link-hover", data: { turbo: "false" } } What it flags
    - steps.with_step(css: "step-primary") do
      %a{ href: "#adopting", class: "link-hover", data: { turbo: "false" } } Adopting
    - steps.with_step(css: "step-primary") do
      %a{ href: "#exceptions", class: "link-hover", data: { turbo: "false" } } Exceptions
    - steps.with_step do
      %a{ href: "#success", class: "link-hover", data: { turbo: "false" } } Success 🎉

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why Lint This At All?
  :markdown
    A `.card` written by hand in a view *works*. That is exactly the problem:
    nothing stops it, so it happens — one hurried template at a time — until
    the app has two ways of rendering every component. The hand-rolled copies
    miss the accessibility attributes the component adds, drift from it as
    DaisyUI renames classes between releases, and cannot pick up improvements
    (a new slot, a Stimulus action, a Turbo option) without being found and
    rewritten individually.

    The library exists so that the markup for a card lives in **one** place.
    Every `daisy_card` call is a promise that the view does not know, or care,
    what classes a card is made of. The linter turns that promise into a
    build step.

.mt-6
  %div{ class: "grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2" }
    %div
      = daisy_badge("Before", css: "badge-ghost mb-2")
      = doc_code(language: "haml") do
        :plain
          .card.bg-base-100.shadow
            .card-body
              %h2.card-title Order #1042
              %p Shipped yesterday.
              .card-actions.justify-end
                %a.btn.btn-primary{ href: order_path(order) } View
    %div
      = daisy_badge("After", css: "badge-primary mb-2")
      = doc_code(language: "haml") do
        :plain
          = daisy_card(title: "Order #1042", css: "bg-base-100 shadow") do |card|
            %p Shipped yesterday.
            - card.with_actions(css: "justify-end") do
              = daisy_button("View", css: "btn-primary", href: order_path(order))

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Run against the **Before** column, the rule reports the `.card`, the
    `.card-body`, and the `.btn`, and each message names the helper to reach
    for. The **After** column passes — and notice that `bg-base-100`, `shadow`,
    `justify-end`, and `btn-primary` are all still there. Utilities and
    modifiers belong in the view; `css:` is how you hand them to the helper.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "setup", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Setup
  :markdown
    The rule ships inside the gem as a linter for
    [haml_lint](https://github.com/sds/haml-lint) and one for
    [erb_lint](https://github.com/Shopify/erb_lint). Both read the **same**
    derived class map, so HAML and ERB views are held to identical rules.

= doc_note(modifier: :warning, title: "The Linters Are Not Installed For You", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    `haml_lint` and `erb_lint` are *development* dependencies of the gem, and
    development dependencies do not propagate to the apps that install it.
    Add whichever one you need to your own `Gemfile`.

.max-w-prose.mt-6
  = daisy_tabs(css: "tabs-lift", radio: true) do |tabs|
    - tabs.with_tab(title: "HAML", checked: true, content_wrapper_css: "pt-2") do
      .prose
        :markdown
          Add the gem, then register the rule in `.haml-lint.yml`. The
          `require:` entry is a plain Ruby `require`, resolved from the gem's
          load path, so there is nothing to copy into your project.

      = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          # Gemfile
          group :development do
            gem "haml_lint", require: false
          end

      = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "yaml") do
        :plain
          # .haml-lint.yml
          require:
            - loco_motion/lint/haml_lint/component_usage

          linters:
            LocoMotionComponentUsage:
              enabled: true

      .prose
        :markdown
          Run it on its own with `--include-linter`, or drop that flag to run
          it alongside whatever haml_lint rules you already use.

      = daisy_code(css: "my-4", prefix: "$") do
        bundle exec haml-lint --include-linter LocoMotionComponentUsage app/views

    - tabs.with_tab(title: "ERB", content_wrapper_css: "pt-2") do
      .prose
        :markdown
          erb_lint loads custom linters from a **directory** rather than a
          config key, so the registration is a one-line shim in
          `.erb_linters/` plus the usual `.erb_lint.yml` entry.

      = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          # Gemfile
          group :development do
            gem "erb_lint", require: false
          end

      = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "ruby") do
        :plain
          # .erb_linters/loco_motion.rb
          require "loco_motion/lint/erb_lint/component_usage"

      = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "yaml") do
        :plain
          # .erb_lint.yml
          linters:
            LocoMotionComponentUsage:
              enabled: true

      = daisy_code(css: "my-4", prefix: "$") do
        bundle exec erb_lint --lint-all --enable-linters LocoMotionComponentUsage

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Either way, the output says what to do rather than merely what is wrong:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4") do |code|
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle exec haml-lint --include-linter LocoMotionComponentUsage app/views
    - code.with_line(prefix: ">", css: "text-warning") do
      app/views/orders/show.html.haml:12 [W] LocoMotionComponentUsage: `.card` is LocoMotion's daisy_card — call the helper instead of hand-rolling the markup

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "flagged", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. What Is (and Isn't) Flagged
  :markdown
    Whether a team trusts a lint rule comes down to its false positives, so
    the rule is deliberately narrow. It reads the **static** classes on a tag
    — the `.card` shorthand and the `class: "card"` attribute spelling alike —
    and reports a class only when it is one a LocoMotion component declares
    as its own.

- # The verdict table: the markup, whether the rule fires, and why.
.mt-4.overflow-x-auto.rounded-box.border.border-base-300
  = daisy_table(css: "table-zebra") do |table|
    - table.with_head do |head|
      - head.with_column { "Markup" }
      - head.with_column { "Flagged?" }
      - head.with_column { "Why" }
    - verdicts.each do |markup, flagged, why|
      - table.with_row do |row|
        - row.with_column do
          %code= markup
        - row.with_column do
          - if flagged
            = daisy_badge("Yes", css: "badge-warning badge-soft")
          - else
            = daisy_badge("No", css: "badge-success badge-soft")
        - row.with_column do
          :markdown
            #{why}

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    The class list is **derived, not hand-maintained**. At load time the rule
    walks `LocoMotion::COMPONENTS` and pairs each component with the classes
    its own `add_css` calls declare, so a component and the rule covering it
    cannot drift apart: a component added in a release is enforced by that
    release. (The hand-written list this replaced had quietly fallen to 25 of
    70 components.) You can inspect the map from a Rails console:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4") do |code|
    - code.with_line(prefix: ">>") do
      require "loco_motion/lint/component_map"
    - code.with_line(prefix: ">>") do
      LocoMotion::Lint::ComponentMap.build.slice("card", "card-body", "drawer-side")
    - code.with_line(prefix: ">") do
      {"card" => "daisy_card", "card-body" => "daisy_card (body part)", "drawer-side" => "daisy_drawer (nested part)"}

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "adopting", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Adopting on an Existing Codebase
  :markdown
    Turn the rule on in an app that has been around for a while and you will
    get a backlog. That is fine — the point is to stop the backlog *growing*
    while it drains, not to convert everything in one sitting. Two ratchets
    work well.

    **Lint only what changed.** Hold new and edited views to the rule and let
    the rest wait their turn. A pre-commit hook or CI step can feed the
    changed HAML files straight to the linter:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4", prefix: "$") do
    git diff --name-only --diff-filter=AMR origin/main... -- '*.haml' | xargs -r bundle exec haml-lint --include-linter LocoMotionComponentUsage

.mt-4.prose
  :markdown
    This is what LocoMotion's own repository does — see
    [`.highball/bin/haml-lint-changed`](https://github.com/profoundry-us/loco_motion/blob/main/.highball/bin/haml-lint-changed),
    which runs the rule against every HAML file an editing session touches.

    **Exclude the backlog by file.** List today's offenders under `exclude:`
    and delete each line as you convert that view. Your own component
    templates belong on this list permanently: components own their markup,
    so raw DaisyUI classes are exactly right there.

.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # .haml-lint.yml
      linters:
        LocoMotionComponentUsage:
          enabled: true
          exclude:
            # Components own their markup — raw classes are correct here.
            - "app/components/**/*.haml"
            # Backlog. Delete each line as the view is converted.
            - "app/views/orders/show.html.haml"
            - "app/views/admin/**/*.haml"

= doc_note(modifier: :warning, title: "--auto-gen-config Is Not a Per-File Ratchet", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    RuboCop's TODO file excludes offenders file by file. haml_lint's
    `--auto-gen-config` only does that while a rule has offences in **15 or
    fewer files** (the `--auto-gen-exclude-limit` default). Past that — which
    is what a real backlog looks like — it writes `enabled: false` for the
    rule instead, switching it off wholesale. Raise the limit
    (`--auto-gen-exclude-limit 500`) or write the exclude list yourself.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "exceptions", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. Deliberate Exceptions
  :markdown
    Sometimes the raw class is the point — a docs page demonstrating what
    `.stack` does, or a snippet the design team asked to see verbatim. Suppress
    the rule the usual way for your template language, and **say why** in the
    same breath. A suppression without a reason is indistinguishable from
    someone who gave up.

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_tabs(css: "tabs-lift", radio: true) do |tabs|
    - tabs.with_tab(title: "HAML", checked: true, content_wrapper_css: "pt-2") do
      = doc_code(language: "haml") do
        :plain
          -# The raw class IS this example: it shows the CSS `stack` used directly.
          -# haml-lint:disable LocoMotionComponentUsage
          .stack.gap-3.w-full
            %img{ src: image_path("one.jpg") }
            %img{ src: image_path("two.jpg") }
          -# haml-lint:enable LocoMotionComponentUsage
    - tabs.with_tab(title: "ERB", content_wrapper_css: "pt-2") do
      = doc_code(language: "erb") do
        :plain
          <%# The raw class IS this example: it shows the CSS `stack` used directly. %>
          <div class="stack gap-3 w-full"><%# erb_lint:disable LocoMotionComponentUsage %>
            <%= image_tag "one.jpg" %>
            <%= image_tag "two.jpg" %>
          </div>

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    If the reason you are reaching for a suppression is "the component can't
    render what I need", that is a gap in the component, not an exception to
    the rule. Extend it — the
    [Building Components guide](#{guide_path("building_components")}) covers
    parts, slots, and the `css:` / `html:` options — or
    [open an issue](https://github.com/profoundry-us/loco_motion/issues) so
    the fix lands for everyone.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "success", class: "scroll-mt-24" } Success!
  :markdown
    The rule is in place and every new view is held to the premise the
    library is built on. A quick checklist before you move on:

.mt-4.flex.flex-col.gap-2
  = daisy_checkbox(name: "lint-gem", css: "checkbox-primary") do |checkbox|
    - checkbox.with_trailing do
      :markdown
        `haml_lint` or `erb_lint` is in your `Gemfile` and the rule is registered.
  = daisy_checkbox(name: "lint-run", css: "checkbox-primary") do |checkbox|
    - checkbox.with_trailing do
      :markdown
        The linter runs in CI or a pre-commit hook — on changed files if you have a backlog.
  = daisy_checkbox(name: "lint-exclude", css: "checkbox-primary") do |checkbox|
    - checkbox.with_trailing do
      :markdown
        Your own `app/components` templates are excluded; components own their markup.
  = daisy_checkbox(name: "lint-reasons", css: "checkbox-primary") do |checkbox|
    - checkbox.with_trailing do
      :markdown
        Every `haml-lint:disable` in the codebase carries a reason.

= daisy_divider

= doc_buttons(css: "mt-8", current_id: params[:id])
